# shell: desktop
# FS-055: a clipboard holding both a cut and a copy is two operations, not
# one.  Treated as a single move, the COPIED file left where it was -- which
# is the one thing a copy promises not to do.
wait 6
note a.txt is cut, then b.txt is added as a copy
click-row 1
wait 2
key x ctrl
wait 3
click-row 2
wait 2
key c ctrl shift
wait 3
note pasted into sub: the cut one moves, the copied one stays where it is
click-row 0
click-row 0
wait 10
expect-title sub
key v ctrl
wait 40
expect-file /tmp/trk_script/sub/a.txt
expect-no-file /tmp/trk_script/a.txt
expect-file /tmp/trk_script/sub/b.txt
expect-file /tmp/trk_script/b.txt
note the copy is still on the clipboard afterwards, so pasting somewhere
note else copies it again rather than finding an empty clipboard
key Up ctrl alt
wait 10
expect-title trk_script
click-row 0
click-row 0
wait 10
expect-title sub
key v ctrl
wait 40
expect-file /tmp/trk_script/b.txt
expect-file /tmp/trk_script/sub/b.txt
quit
